  "query_preview": "..Tranquil weather conditions are taking place across\nnorthern Arizona this afternoon with clear skies and near average\ntemperatures. Humidity remains low, below 15% for most locations,\nwith west-southwest winds at 10 to 25 mph. Typical spring day\nacross the region.\n\nA gradual warming trend continues Friday and Saturday as heights \nincrease and a shortwave ridge tries to develop over the \nSouthwest. By Sunday, temperatures become roughly 5 degrees above \naverage with a slight increase in humidity. Conditions become a \nbit more unstable as well on Sunday in response to an upper-level \nlow pressure system developing off the California coast. Afternoon\nsouthwest winds, 10-20 mph, remain in place through the weekend\nwith slight chances for high-based thunderstorms and/or virga\nshowers to develop over the White Mountains on Sunday.\n\nA bit more weather action is forecast for Memorial Day with\nincreasing cloud cover and scattered showers and hybrid \nthunderstorms thanks to a passing low pressure trough from the\nwest. Atmospheric conditions cool above resulting in instability,\ncoupled with increasing moisture. Precipitation chances are\ngreatest (30-60%) along the high terrain across northern Arizona\nfor Monday afternoon with the biggest threat of dry lightning and\ngusty/erratic outflow winds. This could potentially result in new\nfire starts in regional forests. Even with the increased moisture,\nmodel guidance at this time indicates it will not be enough to \nproduce greater coverage of wetting rains (>0.10\") as the boundary\nlayer should remain on the drier side. \n\nAs we head into Tuesday, temperatures return to near average with\ndrier conditions in place. With the low exiting towards the east\nand another larger/deeper trough develops over the Intermountain \nWest, southwest winds are forecast to become stronger with \nwidespread gusts of 20-30 mph. With these winds and the potential\nfor dry lightning on Monday, the spreading of any new fire starts \nis possible. Something to keep in mind as we enter into peak\nwildfire season for our region.",
